E('layer-angels',
  m=["\\b\\b(?:in detail|detailed|more detail|more about|deeper|tafsir|scholars?|differ\\w*|disagree\\w*|hadiths?|sahih|sources?|evidence|what does the quran say|quran say|ibn kathir|references?)\\b.*\\b(?:angels?|jibril|gabriel|israfil|mikail)\\b", "\\b\\b(?:angels?|jibril|gabriel|israfil|mikail)\\b.*\\b(?:in detail|detailed|more detail|more about|deeper|tafsir|scholars?|differ\\w*|disagree\\w*|hadiths?|sahih|sources?|evidence|what does the quran say|quran say|ibn kathir|references?)\\b", "\\b(?:what do you know about|tell me about|who are|what are|explain) (?:the )?(?:angels?|jibril|gabriel|israfil|mikail)\\b"],
  topic="The angels",
  go=['/stories'],
  text="""
The angels: what the app's checked sources hold, in detail.

In the Qur'an:
- Jibril and Mika’il are named among the angels. ({QR 2:98})
- Angels as messengers with wings, two or three or four. ({QR 35:1})
- They prostrate and are not arrogant; they do what they are commanded. ({QR 16:49-50})
- Honoured servants who act by His command. ({QR 21:26-28})
- The angels over the Fire do not disobey. ({QR 66:6})
- The noble recording keepers. ({QR 82:10-12})

In the hadith:
- {HS muslim:2996|Aisha reported the Prophet ﷺ saying the angels were created from light.}
- {HS bukhari:3232|Ibn Mas’ud said that the Prophet ﷺ saw Jibril with six hundred wings, explaining the verses of Surat al-Najm about the vision.}
- {HS muslim:174|The same report of Ibn Mas’ud in Sahih Muslim, in the chapter on the Lote-Tree of the Utmost Boundary: the Prophet ﷺ saw Jibril, who had six hundred wings.}
- In the account of the night of the ascent, the Prophet ﷺ was shown al-Bayt al-Ma’mur in the seventh heaven: seventy thousand angels pray there each day, and those who leave never return. ({H bukhari:3207})
- {HS abudawud:4727|The Prophet ﷺ said he had been permitted to speak of an angel among the bearers of the Throne, the distance from the lobe of whose ear to his shoulder is a journey of seven hundred years.}
- Graders differ (al-Tirmidhi (hasan gharib); also Hasan per al-Albani and Darussalam): Abu Dharr reported the Prophet ﷺ saying he saw what others do not see and heard what they do not hear: the heaven moans, and rightly so, for there is no spot the size of four fingers where an angel is not prostrating. The report is in the chapter on “if you knew what I know you would laugh little”. Graded hasan, not sahih, by al-Tirmidhi, al-Albani and Darussalam; handle it as a hasan report. ({H tirmidhi:2312|Abu Dharr reported the Prophet ﷺ saying he saw what others do not see and heard what they do not hear: the heaven moans, and rightly so, for there is no spot the size of four fingers where an angel is not prostrating. The report is in the chapter on “if you knew what I know you would laugh little”.})
- {HS bukhari:3208|Ibn Mas’ud reported the Prophet ﷺ describing the stages of the child in the womb, after which an angel is sent and commanded to write four things: the person’s deeds, provision, lifespan, and whether they will be wretched or blessed.}
- {HS bukhari:3223|Abu Huraira reported that angels take turns among you by night and by day and meet at the Fajr and Asr prayers; those who stayed with you go up, and Allah asks them how they left His servants.}
- {HS bukhari:6408|Abu Huraira reported that Allah has angels who roam the roads looking for people who remember Allah, and who report on them to Him.}
- {HS muslim:2842|Ibn Mas’ud reported that on the Day of Judgment Hell will be brought with seventy thousand bridles, each pulled by seventy thousand angels.}
- Aisha asked the Prophet ﷺ about the hardest day he had known. He described the rejection at Ta’if; Jibril appeared in a cloud and the Angel of the Mountains offered to act against the people, but the Prophet ﷺ declined, hoping Allah would bring from them those who worship Him alone. ({H bukhari:3231})

What the scholars say:
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 2:97-98: Calls Jibril “Ruh al-Qudus”, who brought the Qur’an to the Prophet’s ﷺ heart by Allah’s permission, and who carries out his missions by his Lord’s command and not on his own. It names Israfil as the one entrusted with blowing the Trumpet, and says that whoever is an enemy to any of them is an enemy to Allah.
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 35:1: Says the angels are messengers with wings, some with two, some three, some four, and some more, and cites the report that the Prophet ﷺ saw Jibril with six hundred wings (the page does not name its compiler).

Where scholars differ:
- Who were Harut and Marut (2:102)? Many of the Salaf, via al-Qurtubi: They were two angels sent down to earth as a trial. The abridged text names, among others, Ibn Mas’ud and Ibn Abbas. To reconcile this with angels not disobeying Allah (66:6), Ibn Kathir argues that Allah knew in His eternal knowledge what they would do, and compares Iblis.; A view quoted in the same passage: They were two ordinary men, not angels. Ibn Kathir states that this explanation “is not plausible”.. The Qur’an says only that they taught people while saying “we are a trial, so do not disbelieve”. The abridged text does not discuss the tale of a woman named Zuhrah, so no verdict on it is reported here.

The story itself, chapter by chapter, is in Stories.
""",
  review='Scholar review needed: built from the app\'s story layer (checked 2026-10-20); scholars to confirm the selection and wording.')

E('layer-jinn',
  m=["\\b\\b(?:in detail|detailed|more detail|more about|deeper|tafsir|scholars?|differ\\w*|disagree\\w*|hadiths?|sahih|sources?|evidence|what does the quran say|quran say|ibn kathir|references?)\\b.*\\b(?:jinn|jinns|djinn)\\b", "\\b\\b(?:jinn|jinns|djinn)\\b.*\\b(?:in detail|detailed|more detail|more about|deeper|tafsir|scholars?|differ\\w*|disagree\\w*|hadiths?|sahih|sources?|evidence|what does the quran say|quran say|ibn kathir|references?)\\b", "\\b(?:what do you know about|tell me about|who are|what are|explain) (?:the )?(?:jinn|jinns|djinn)\\b"],
  topic="The jinn",
  go=['/stories'],
  text="""
The jinn: what the app's checked sources hold, in detail.

In the Qur'an:
- Mankind from clay; the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire. ({QR 55:14-15})
- Iblis “was of the jinn”. ({QR 18:50})
- Satan and his tribe see us from where we do not see them. ({QR 7:27})
- A group of jinn listen to the Qur’an and return to their people as warners. ({QR 46:29-31})
- The jinn’s own words about what they heard. ({QR 72:1-6})
- When Sulayman died, the jinn learned they did not know the unseen. ({QR 34:14})

In the hadith:
- The Prophet ﷺ said, in Aisha’s report, that the angels were created from light, the jinn from a flame of fire, and Adam from what has been described to you in the Qur’an. ({H muslim:2996})
- {HS muslim:2814|The Prophet ﷺ said every person has a companion from the jinn assigned to them. Asked whether this applied to him too, he said yes, but Allah helped him against it so that it submitted and commands him only to good.}
- {HS bukhari:4921|Ibn Abbas reported that the devils were barred from hearing news of heaven and sent flames, and went searching the earth. Some came upon the Prophet ﷺ reciting in the Fajr prayer, were moved by the Qur’an and returned to their people; Surat al-Jinn was then revealed about it.}
- {HS muslim:449|The same report of Ibn Abbas in Sahih Muslim: the jinn overheard the Prophet ﷺ reciting in the dawn prayer on the way to Ukaz; he neither recited to them nor saw them.}
- {HS muslim:450|Ibn Mas’ud reported a night when the Prophet ﷺ was missing; he later explained that a summoner from the jinn had come and he had gone to recite the Qur’an to them. They asked for provision and he told them bones over which Allah’s name had been mentioned and animal dung would be their food, so he forbade using them to clean oneself. Ibn Mas’ud was not present that night.}
- {HS bukhari:3860|Abu Huraira carried water for the Prophet ﷺ, who asked him for stones and not for bones or dung, saying these are the food of the jinn. He said a delegation of jinn from Nasibin had come to him and asked for provision, and he prayed for them.}

What the scholars say:
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 18:50: Says Iblis was of the jinn and not an angel, quoting al-Hasan al-Basri that he was never an angel even for a moment, and says his original nature betrayed him. It supports created from fire with Qur’an 38:76 and a hadith of Aisha, and notes the angels were created from light.
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 6:130: Concludes that messengers are only from mankind, attributing this to Mujahid, Ibn Jurayj and others of the Salaf and later scholars, and says no one has claimed that there were prophets from among the jinn. The jinn follow mankind in this and have warners, as in 46:29-32.
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 46:29-32: Records that after the first listening the jinn came to the Prophet ﷺ in groups, one party after another, as successive delegations.
- Ibn Kathir, Tafsir Ibn Kathir, on 34:14: Says that when Sulayman fell it became clear that the jinn do not know the unseen, as people had imagined.

Where scholars differ:
- Did the Prophet ﷺ meet the jinn knowingly? The report of Ibn Abbas (Sahih Muslim 449; the same event in Sahih al-Bukhari 4921): He did not recite to the jinn and did not see them. They overheard the Fajr prayer on the way to Ukaz.; The report of Ibn Mas’ud (Sahih Muslim 450): He went out at night in answer to a summoner from the jinn and recited the Qur’an to them. Ibn Mas’ud himself was not there.. Both are in Muslim. Ibn Kathir (abridged), on 46:29-32, records both reports and says the jinn afterwards came to him in successive delegations. The abridged text does not say more than this about how the reports fit together.
- Was Iblis an angel before his disobedience? Al-Hasan al-Basri: Iblis was not one of the angels, not even for a moment; he was the origin of the jinn as Adam is of mankind.; Ibn Abbas, reported through Muhammad ibn Ishaq: He was from a kind called the jinn, but before his sin he was among the angels and was called Azazil.. Qur’an 18:50 says he “was of the jinn”. Ibn Kathir’s own conclusion in the abridged text is that Iblis was not an angel.

The story itself, chapter by chapter, is in Stories.
""",
  review='Scholar review needed: built from the app\'s story layer (checked 2026-10-20); scholars to confirm the selection and wording.')
